**Streams that MUST be closed (hold OS resources):**

```java
// Files.lines() — opens BufferedReader → file handle
Stream<String> lines = Files.lines(Path.of("/bank/transactions.csv"));
// (Caution) If not closed: file handle leaks!

// Files.walk() — opens DirectoryStream recursively
Stream<Path> files = Files.walk(Path.of("/bank/reports"), 3);
// (Caution) If not closed: directory handle leaks!

// Files.list() — opens DirectoryStream (one level)
Stream<Path> entries = Files.list(Path.of("/bank/logs"));

// JDBC ResultSet streams (some libraries)
Stream<Trade> dbStream = jdbcTemplate.queryForStream("SELECT * FROM trades", mapper);
```

**Streams that do NOT need closing (in-memory):**

```java
Collection.stream()           // reads from in-memory List/Set/Map
Arrays.stream(array)          // reads from in-memory array
Stream.of(elements)           // wraps varargs
Stream.generate(supplier)     // calls supplier — no OS resource
Stream.iterate(seed, fn)      // pure computation
IntStream.range(0, 100)       // generates numbers — no OS resource
```

**The leak — what happens without closing:**

```java
// LEAK! File handle never released:
public List<String> readTransactions() throws IOException {
    return Files.lines(Path.of("transactions.csv"))  // opens file handle
        .filter(line -> line.contains("SETTLED"))
        .toList();  // terminal completes, but file handle NOT released!
    // The GC might eventually close it, but no guarantee of WHEN.
    // Under load: 1000 requests → 1000 open handles → "Too many open files"
}
```

**The fix — try-with-resources:**

```java
// SAFE! File handle closed even if exception occurs:
public List<String> readTransactions() throws IOException {
    try (Stream<String> lines = Files.lines(Path.of("transactions.csv"))) {
        return lines
            .filter(line -> line.contains("SETTLED"))
            .toList();
    }  // ← file handle closed HERE, guaranteed (AutoCloseable)
}
```

**Stream.onClose() — register cleanup callback:**

```java
Stream<String> lines = Files.lines(path)
    .onClose(() -> log.info("File handle released for {}", path));
// When stream.close() is called (by try-with-resources), onClose callback fires
```

